  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-7239",
      "knowledge_point_id": "0b70a3b7-90a7-5a83-b71f-304f7f25c4ef",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/1ca11413bbce2ff307e494fb5a79028535b76cff24619e6921620f88c3e274ec.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({2}^{x})+c.$  Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve.",
      "worked_solution": "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({2}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-1,$ so $c=-1.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({2}^{x})-1.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,2),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=2$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({2}^{x})-1 \\\\ 2 & =k({2}^{0})-1 \\\\ 2 & =k(1)-1 \\\\ 2 & =k-1 \\\\ 3 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=3({2}^{x})-1.$",
